            <ArticleTitle>Hypertensive therapy--effect on coronary risk factors.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>26-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Antihypertensive agents, particularly diuretics and beta-blockers, can &quot;worsen&quot; certain coronary risk factors, e.g. plasma lipids, which could theoretically offset the benefit of lowering blood pressure. However, the use of such surrogate markers may be inappropriate; propranolol has been shown to significantly decrease the frequency of myocardial infarction (MI) as evidenced by the appearance of Q-waves on ECG, and diuretics have significantly reduced the frequency of clinical MI in the elderly patient with isolated systolic hypertension.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
